{"id":"neet-2026-q023","pn":23,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Photoelectric cells stopping potential","q":"A ray of light with wavelength λ is incident on three different photoelectric cells namely 1, 2 and 3. The threshold wavelength of these photoelectric cells are λ₁, λ₂, and λ₃, respectively and the magnitude of stopping potentials of these cells are V₁, V₂ and V₃, respectively. The relation between λ and threshold wavelengths are λ₁ < λ, λ₂ > λ and λ₃ >> λ. The correct option is:","o":["V₁ = 0, V₂ > V₃","V₁ > V₂, V₃ = 0","V₁ < V₂, V₃ = 0","V₁ = 0, V₂ < V₃"],"c":3,"s":"An electron is ejected only if the photon energy is greater than the work function, φ = hc/λ.\nFor cell 1, λ₁ < λ, so φ₁ > φ (work function of cell 1 is greater than the photon energy).\nSo no electron is ejected and V₁ = 0.\nEinstein equation: photon energy φ = φ₀ + eV.\nFor cells 2 and 3: λ₂ > λ and λ₃ >> λ, so φ > φ₂ and φ >> φ₃ (work functions are smaller than photon energy).\nSo V₃ > V₂."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q040","pn":40,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Momentum of photon and electron","q":"A photon and an electron, each of 20 eV energy, move in free space. The ratio of linear momentum of electron pₑ to that of photon pPh, pₑ/pPh is:\n[Take speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹, charge of electron = –1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mass of electron = 9 × 10⁻³¹ kg]","o":["1/250","225","275","2/450"],"c":1,"s":"Eₑ = EPh = 20 eV.\nFor the electron pₑ = √(2mₑEₑ), for the photon pPh = EPh/c.\npₑ/pPh = (√(2mₑEₑ)/EPh)·c = √(2mₑ/EPh)·c.\npₑ/pPh = √((2 × 9 × 10⁻³¹)/(20 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)) × 3 × 10⁸.\n= (3/4) × 10⁻⁶ × 3 × 10⁸.\n= 900/4 = 225."}
